  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Shelf-Edge Ticket",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"The Shelf-Edge Ticket\"**\n\nA competitor's price change is a price ticket clipped to a supermarket shelf edge, and a cut wears a yellow markdown sticker. The evidence is the ticket itself, not a summary tile: white card-stock tickets hang on a grey shelf rail, each showing the old price struck through and the new price in big condensed figures. Everything around the tickets is quiet working paper: white cards on a pale shelf ground, carbon ink, and a carbon top bar.\n\nThe system is light and daylight-legible, built for a merchant reading next to their own store admin. Density is that of a working tool: tables over decorative cards, exact numbers everywhere, tabular figures throughout. Colour is information, never decoration: yellow means a cut (or a numbered action), red means a dropped price, and the green, amber and blue soft tags mean verification status. It rejects the category default of KPI tiles on a grey SaaS shell and the earlier dark zinc look.\n\nMotion is limited to 150ms state transitions (colour, opacity, a 2px ticket lift on hover). There is no entrance choreography, and reduced-motion collapses all transitions.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Price tickets on a grey rail are the unit for price changes everywhere (home and brief detail).",
      "Barlow for UI and reading; Barlow Condensed 800 for week titles, prices and big numbers.",
      "Tabular figures on the whole document.",
      "Carbon top bar; white card stock with a 1px edge on a shelf-grey ground.",
      "Yellow and red are reserved signals, not brand accents."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Sticker Means a Cut Rule",
        "body": "Yellow appears only on real price cuts, the numbered action markers and the active-tab / hover affordances of the top bar. A price rise gets a carbon sticker. Yellow is never a decorative highlight, badge fill or section accent.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Red Is a Drop Rule",
        "body": "Reduction red marks a lower price (and a form error). Never use it for warnings, failure states or emphasis; failures are amber.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Condensed Is for Numbers and Titles Rule",
        "body": "Barlow Condensed carries prices, percentages on stickers, action numbers, week and section titles. Running text, table cells and labels stay in Barlow.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Rule",
        "body": "Figures are tabular across the whole document so columns of prices and counts align.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Only Ticket Lifts Rule",
        "body": "Containers are bordered, not shadowed. The ticket shadow belongs to tickets; giving it to cards turns every surface into evidence.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do show every price change as a ticket on the grey rail, with the new price in Barlow Condensed 800 and the old price struck through.",
      "Do put the yellow sticker only on real cuts; a rise gets the carbon sticker.",
      "Do keep reduction red for lower prices and form errors, and use amber for failures and warnings.",
      "Do keep cards flat with a 1px edge border; reserve the ticket shadow for tickets.",
      "Do keep figures tabular and right-aligned in tables.",
      "Do state empty and baseline states in one plain sentence on a shelf-grey inset.",
      "Do keep transitions at 150ms on colour, opacity and the ticket lift, with no entrance animation."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't build KPI tiles or summary stat cards; the ticket and the table are the evidence.",
      "Don't use yellow as a decorative highlight, section accent or badge fill.",
      "Don't give cards, tables or tags the ticket shadow or any rotation.",
      "Don't set running text, table cells or labels in Barlow Condensed.",
      "Don't return to a dark theme shell; the system is light only."
    ]
  }
